{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cg3MihQC8GF6w4wW6jfJx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UrnrDB8oNwpcfFrwL5wzuA","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/Users/nick/code/microtags.hydration-dx-fixes-12-prototype-render-support-registration/docs/adr/ADR-009-server-render-support-through-render-link.md","content":"# ADR-009: Server render support loaded through the render link\n\n**Date:** 2026-10-14\n\n**Status:** Accepted\n\nAmends [ADR-005](./ADR-005-single-file-components-with-build-time-render-stripping.md).\n\n## Context\n\nADR-005 says a browser bundle holds hydration code only. That is not true\ntoday. `withProps` computes a serializer for every prop, and `setup`\ninstalls a `render` static that closes over the host-tag builder\n(`_renderHost`), the serializers (`_serializerFor` and friends) and\nattribute escaping (`_escAttr`). All of it is reachable from `define()`,\nso a bundler keeps it even when the build transform has stripped every\n`.render` link. Core grew from 3.48 kB to 4.17 kB to carry this code. On\nthe hydration example, stripping saves only 3% of the client bundle\n(review of 2026-10-13, finding 8).\n\nThe fix is to move that code into a separate server render support\nmodule. Core keeps the prop metadata (kind and coercer) and the render\nfunction, and installs a minimal `render` static that delegates to the\nsupport module. The open question was how the support module registers\nitself with core so that an all-stripped client never reaches it.\nThree mechanisms were prototyped:\n\n- **A. Side-effect subpath.** Importing `microtags/render-support` writes\n  its render function into a slot object exported by a small core module.\n- **B. Setter exported from core.** Core exports `setRenderSupport(fn)`,\n  and the caller passes in the function the support subpath exports. It\n  does nothing on import.\n- **C. Module-level hook.** Importing the support subpath sets the render\n  function on `globalThis` under `Symbol.for('microtags.render')`. Core's\n  delegating `render` reads it from there.\n\nIn all three, both templaters load the support module, and calling\n`render` before registration throws an error that names the module to\nimport.\n\n## Measurements\n\nThe prototype lives in `.scratch/hydration-dx-fixes/prototype-12/`, and\n`measure.sh` there reproduces every number below. It used the review's\nmethod: each entry is bundled minified as ESM, then `size-limit` (preset\nsmall-lib, min + brotli) measures the result. The baseline row matches\nthe review's numbers exactly. The fixtures sit in the same directory.\n`all-stripped.ts` defines two components with number, string, boolean\nand JSON props and has no render link and no templater import.\n`kept.ts` is the same code with one render link kept, plus the import\nthe transform would inject.\n\n| Variant | core | `htm` | `htm` browser | all-stripped | kept |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Baseline (HEAD) | 4.17 kB | 1.43 kB | 4.56 kB | 3.83 kB | 3.87 kB |\n| A. Side-effect subpath | 3.90 kB | 1.82 kB | 4.91 kB | 3.61 kB | 3.98 kB |\n| B. Setter from core | 3.91 kB | 1.85 kB | 4.92 kB | 3.60 kB | 3.96 kB |\n| C. Global hook (chosen) | 3.93 kB | 1.83 kB | 4.93 kB | 3.63 kB | 4.00 kB |\n\nFor every variant, the all-stripped fixture's esbuild metafile lists no\nsupport module input, and the kept fixture's does. At the baseline, the\nsame code is in `builders.ts`, which every bundle includes.\n\nHow to read the columns:\n\n- **core** loses the host-tag builder, serializers and escaping: -0.24\n  kB with C.\n- The **templater** entries are self-contained bundles. They now carry\n  the support code that used to sit in core, so each grows by about\n  0.35 kB. An app that bundles core and the browser templater together\n  pays for the support code once, as before.\n- **all-stripped** is the win: -0.20 kB with C, and the support code is\n  gone.\n- **kept** costs a little more than before: +0.13 kB with C. That pays\n  for the delegating static, the render record and the error message.\n  Ticket 13 should shorten the message.\n\nThe three variants are within 0.03 kB of each other on every entry.\n\n## Decision\n\nUse **C, the module-level hook.** The support module is the subpath\n`microtags/render-support`. When it is evaluated, it sets\n`globalThis[Symbol.for('microtags.render')]` to its render function. A\ncomponent's `render` static looks the function up on each call and\nthrows if it is missing:\n\n- The server templater (`microtags/htm`) imports the support module\n  statically.\n- The browser templater does the same. The hook is set when the\n  templater module is evaluated, before it can create any host.\n- The `microtags/build` transform injects\n  `import 'microtags/render-support'` into any client module where it\n  keeps a render link. It injects nothing where every link was stripped.\n- Code that calls a component's `render` static directly, with no\n  templater, must import `microtags/render-support` once first.\n\nWhy the others lost:\n\n- **A** breaks when core and the templater come from different copies of\n  the package. The README documents an import map in which each subpath\n  maps to its own self-contained `.min.js` file, so `microtags` and\n  `microtags/htm` each carry their own copy of the slot. The templater\n  fills its copy, and core's `render` still throws. The prototype shows\n  this with two copies of `src/`: A throws and C renders. Two-copy setups\n  are already a known problem here (the Markup brand fix uses\n  `Symbol.for` for the same reason). A saves 0.03 kB over C, which does\n  not justify the failure.\n- **B** has the same two-copy problem, unless the caller registers with\n  the right copy by hand. It also makes every registration site longer.\n  The transform would have to inject an import of core, an import of the\n  support module and a call. Those names could collide with the user's\n  own, and the cascade would have to treat the injected code specially. A\n  direct caller would need three lines instead of one. It also adds a\n  public export to core.\n\n## Trade-offs and consequences\n\n**Breaking change.** Code that calls a component's `render` static\nwithout going through a templater must now import\n`microtags/render-support` once, for example in a Hono route that calls\n`Counter.render(props)` directly. Without that import, `render` throws an\nerror naming the module. This is acceptable at 0.0.x and the README\ndocuments it (ticket 13).\n\n**Who wins.** Only apps that strip every render link and import no\ntemplater in the client get the saving. The hydration example keeps\nBadge and Notice (`client: true`) and imports the browser templater, so\nits client still ships the support module, and its size barely moves.\nAn app that keeps even one render link pays about 0.13 kB more than\ntoday.\n\n**Global state.** The hook is one global property. Two different\nlibrary versions on one page share it, and the last support module to\nload wins. This makes the render record that core passes (tag, prop\nmetadata, ref names, render function) a contract between versions. A\nbreaking change to that shape must change the symbol key, for example to\n`microtags.render.2`.\n\n**Side effects.** Registration relies on bundlers keeping a bare\nside-effect import. `package.json` has no `sideEffects` field today. If\none is ever added, it must list the support module. The cascade already\nkeeps imports with no import clause.\n\n**Budgets.** The prototype puts core at 3.93 kB, so the core budget\nfalls from 4.2 kB to 3.95 kB. Ticket 13 measures the real change and\nsets the final limit, rounded up to the next 0.05 kB, and records it\nhere. The browser templater measures 4.93 kB against its 4.6 kB limit.\nUnder the spec's rule its limit rises to the measured size, rounded up\nto the next 0.05 kB, and ADR-007's note records it.\n\n**Docs.** ADR-005's claims about bundle contents and the core budget are\namended by this record. AGENTS.md's \"Builder and serialization\" section\nmust be rewritten in ticket 13, because the serializer, escape function\nand host-tag wrapper no longer live in core. New coercer types then need\na serializer branch in the support module.\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":623,"cache_read_input_tokens":96648,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":623},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"871ab629-50f7-4bcf-b98c-3172e797b7d3","uuid":"2475400a-9c1d-407c-9a0c-f17a420510a8","timestamp":"2026-10-15T04:03:20.037Z","request_id":"req_011Cg3Mih7L7wS3zwYhAk44i","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01UrnrDB8oNwpcfFrwL5wzuA":{"file_path":"/Users/nick/code/microtags.hydration-dx-fixes-12-prototype-render-support-registration/docs/adr/ADR-009-server-render-support-through-render-link.md","content":"# ADR-009: Server render support loaded through the render link\n\n**Date:** 2026-10-14\n\n**Status:** Accepted\n\nAmends [ADR-005](./ADR-005-single-file-components-with-build-time-render-stripping.md).\n\n## Context\n\nADR-005 says a browser bundle holds hydration code only. That is not true\ntoday. `withProps` computes a serializer for every prop, and `setup`\ninstalls a `render` static that closes over the host-tag builder\n(`_renderHost`), the serializers (`_serializerFor` and friends) and\nattribute escaping (`_escAttr`). All of it is reachable from `define()`,\nso a bundler keeps it even when the build transform has stripped every\n`.render` link. Core grew from 3.48 kB to 4.17 kB to carry this code. On\nthe hydration example, stripping saves only 3% of the client bundle\n(review of 2026-10-13, finding 8).\n\nThe fix is to move that code into a separate server render support\nmodule. Core keeps the prop metadata (kind and coercer) and the render\nfunction, and installs a minimal `render` static that delegates to the\nsupport module. The open question was how the support module registers\nitself with core so that an all-stripped client never reaches it.\nThree mechanisms were prototyped:\n\n- **A. Side-effect subpath.** Importing `microtags/render-support` writes\n  its render function into a slot object exported by a small core module.\n- **B. Setter exported from core.** Core exports `setRenderSupport(fn)`,\n  and the caller passes in the function the support subpath exports. It\n  does nothing on import.\n- **C. Module-level hook.** Importing the support subpath sets the render\n  function on `globalThis` under `Symbol.for('microtags.render')`. Core's\n  delegating `render` reads it from there.\n\nIn all three, both templaters load the support module, and calling\n`render` before registration throws an error that names the module to\nimport.\n\n## Measurements\n\nThe prototype lives in `.scratch/hydration-dx-fixes/prototype-12/`, and\n`measure.sh` there reproduces every number below. It used the review's\nmethod: each entry is bundled minified as ESM, then `size-limit` (preset\nsmall-lib, min + brotli) measures the result. The baseline row matches\nthe review's numbers exactly. The fixtures sit in the same directory.\n`all-stripped.ts` defines two components with number, string, boolean\nand JSON props and has no render link and no templater import.\n`kept.ts` is the same code with one render link kept, plus the import\nthe transform would inject.\n\n| Variant | core | `htm` | `htm` browser | all-stripped | kept |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Baseline (HEAD) | 4.17 kB | 1.43 kB | 4.56 kB | 3.83 kB | 3.87 kB |\n| A. Side-effect subpath | 3.90 kB | 1.82 kB | 4.91 kB | 3.61 kB | 3.98 kB |\n| B. Setter from core | 3.91 kB | 1.85 kB | 4.92 kB | 3.60 kB | 3.96 kB |\n| C. Global hook (chosen) | 3.93 kB | 1.83 kB | 4.93 kB | 3.63 kB | 4.00 kB |\n\nFor every variant, the all-stripped fixture's esbuild metafile lists no\nsupport module input, and the kept fixture's does. At the baseline, the\nsame code is in `builders.ts`, which every bundle includes.\n\nHow to read the columns:\n\n- **core** loses the host-tag builder, serializers and escaping: -0.24\n  kB with C.\n- The **templater** entries are self-contained bundles. They now carry\n  the support code that used to sit in core, so each grows by about\n  0.35 kB. An app that bundles core and the browser templater together\n  pays for the support code once, as before.\n- **all-stripped** is the win: -0.20 kB with C, and the support code is\n  gone.\n- **kept** costs a little more than before: +0.13 kB with C. That pays\n  for the delegating static, the render record and the error message.\n  Ticket 13 should shorten the message.\n\nThe three variants are within 0.03 kB of each other on every entry.\n\n## Decision\n\nUse **C, the module-level hook.** The support module is the subpath\n`microtags/render-support`. When it is evaluated, it sets\n`globalThis[Symbol.for('microtags.render')]` to its render function. A\ncomponent's `render` static looks the function up on each call and\nthrows if it is missing:\n\n- The server templater (`microtags/htm`) imports the support module\n  statically.\n- The browser templater does the same. The hook is set when the\n  templater module is evaluated, before it can create any host.\n- The `microtags/build` transform injects\n  `import 'microtags/render-support'` into any client module where it\n  keeps a render link. It injects nothing where every link was stripped.\n- Code that calls a component's `render` static directly, with no\n  templater, must import `microtags/render-support` once first.\n\nWhy the others lost:\n\n- **A** breaks when core and the templater come from different copies of\n  the package. The README documents an import map in which each subpath\n  maps to its own self-contained `.min.js` file, so `microtags` and\n  `microtags/htm` each carry their own copy of the slot. The templater\n  fills its copy, and core's `render` still throws. The prototype shows\n  this with two copies of `src/`: A throws and C renders. Two-copy setups\n  are already a known problem here (the Markup brand fix uses\n  `Symbol.for` for the same reason). A saves 0.03 kB over C, which does\n  not justify the failure.\n- **B** has the same two-copy problem, unless the caller registers with\n  the right copy by hand. It also makes every registration site longer.\n  The transform would have to inject an import of core, an import of the\n  support module and a call. Those names could collide with the user's\n  own, and the cascade would have to treat the injected code specially. A\n  direct caller would need three lines instead of one. It also adds a\n  public export to core.\n\n## Trade-offs and consequences\n\n**Breaking change.** Code that calls a component's `render` static\nwithout going through a templater must now import\n`microtags/render-support` once, for example in a Hono route that calls\n`Counter.render(props)` directly. Without that import, `render` throws an\nerror naming the module. This is acceptable at 0.0.x and the README\ndocuments it (ticket 13).\n\n**Who wins.** Only apps that strip every render link and import no\ntemplater in the client get the saving. The hydration example keeps\nBadge and Notice (`client: true`) and imports the browser templater, so\nits client still ships the support module, and its size barely moves.\nAn app that keeps even one render link pays about 0.13 kB more than\ntoday.\n\n**Global state.** The hook is one global property. Two different\nlibrary versions on one page share it, and the last support module to\nload wins. This makes the render record that core passes (tag, prop\nmetadata, ref names, render function) a contract between versions. A\nbreaking change to that shape must change the symbol key, 